Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the "ability to consummate" concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: "Company Material Adverse Effect" means any condition, event, change, or effect that, individually or in the aggregate, materially impairs the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement or has a material adverse effect on the business, financial condition, or results of operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries, taken as a whole; provided, however, that the following shall not be considered a Company Material Adverse Effect: (i) changes in global economic conditions or in the financial markets generally; (ii) changes in the industries in which the Company operates; (iii) changes in Law or GAAP; (iv) any acts of war, terrorism, or natural disasters; (v) the announcement or pendency of this Agreement; (vi) compliance with the terms of this Agreement, including any action taken with the consent of the other party; (vii) the failure to meet any internal or public projections, forecasts, or estimates; (viii) changes in stock price or trading volume; provided further, that with respect to clauses (i), (ii), and (iv), such effects shall be considered only if they disproportionately affect the Company compared to other companies in the same industry.\n\nAnswer:
Choices:

